# ScanBase - SBOM Manager Development Rules

## Core Principles

### SOLID & KISS
- Follow SOLID principles but keep it simple (KISS)
- Avoid over-engineering - prefer simple, straightforward solutions
- Code should be easy to understand and maintain
- Split code into logical modules with single responsibility
- Use clear, descriptive file and function names

### Code Organization
- Backend: Use modular structure in `lib/` directory
  - `lib/db.js` - Database operations and schema
  - `lib/github.js` - GitHub API client
  - `lib/osv.js` - OSV.dev API client and vulnerability processing
  - `lib/sbom.js` - SBOM parsing logic
  - `lib/auth.js` - Authentication helpers
  - `lib/utils.js` - Shared utilities (logging, etc.)
- Frontend: Separate HTML, CSS, and JavaScript
  - `public/index.html` - HTML structure only
  - `public/style.css` - All styles with CSS variables
  - `public/app.js` - Alpine.js application logic
- Routes: `routes.js` - All API route handlers

### Dependencies
- Prefer core Node.js features when possible
- Use established, well-maintained packages when needed:
  - `express` - HTTP server and routing
  - `better-sqlite3` - SQLite database
  - `dotenv` - Environment variable management
  - `alpinejs` - Frontend reactivity (CDN)
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies - keep the stack minimal

## Technical Standards

### Node.js
- Target Node.js v24
- Use modern JavaScript features (async/await, arrow functions, const/let)
- Use ES modules or CommonJS consistently

### Database
- Use `better-sqlite3` for SQLite
- Initialize schema in `lib/db.js`
- Use prepared statements for all queries
- Use transactions for bulk operations
- Include proper indexes for performance
- Handle NULL values with COALESCE when needed

### API Design
- RESTful endpoints under `/api/`
- Use Express.js for routing
- Return JSON responses with consistent structure
- Include proper error handling and status codes
- Support authentication via cookies (GitHub OAuth)

### Frontend
- Use Alpine.js for reactivity and state management
- Keep HTML semantic and accessible
- Use CSS variables for theming (light/dark mode)
- Support dark mode via `prefers-color-scheme`
- Ensure responsive design and good UX

### Styling
- Use CSS variables for all colors
- Define variables in `:root` for light mode
- Override in `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)` for dark mode
- Maintain good contrast ratios for accessibility
- Use consistent spacing and typography

### Error Handling
- Always handle errors gracefully
- Provide meaningful error messages to users
- Log errors appropriately (use DEBUG flag for verbose logging)
- Validate input on both client and server
- Handle edge cases (null values, missing data, etc.)

### Security
- Require authentication for user-specific data
- Validate and sanitize all user inputs
- Use parameterized queries (prepared statements)
- Handle authentication tokens securely (cookies)
- Support GitHub OAuth for authentication

## Code Style

### JavaScript
- Use async/await for asynchronous operations
- Use descriptive variable and function names
- Add comments for complex logic
- Keep functions focused and small
- Use early returns for error cases
- Prefer const over let, avoid var

### SQL
- Use prepared statements
- Include proper indexes
- Use COALESCE for NULL handling
- Group related queries logically
- Comment complex queries

### CSS
- Use CSS variables for all values
- Organize by component/feature
- Use semantic class names
- Keep specificity low
- Support both light and dark modes

### HTML
- Keep structure semantic
- Use Alpine.js directives for interactivity
- Minimize inline styles (use CSS classes)
- Ensure accessibility (labels, ARIA where needed)

## Features

### SBOM Management
- Support CycloneDX and SPDX JSON formats
- Fetch SBOM from GitHub dependency graph API
- Parse and display dependencies
- Support scanning from GitHub repositories

### Vulnerability Analysis
- Integrate with OSV.dev API
- Support PURL-based queries
- Infer ecosystem when PURL missing
- Normalize versions for better matching
- Batch queries for efficiency
- Enrich with full vulnerability details when needed
- Display severity levels (critical, high, medium, low)
- Show vulnerability counts and details

### Data Persistence
- Store scans, dependencies, and vulnerabilities in SQLite
- Maintain history per user and repository
- Support multiple scans per repository
- Deduplicate dependencies across scans
- Load from database when available

### Search
- Support regex pattern matching across repositories
- Search in dependency name, version, PURL, and group
- Show vulnerability information in results
- Deduplicate results per repository/ref
- Display repository and scan information

### User Interface
- Two-page structure: list view and details view
- Show scanned repositories with statistics
- Display dependencies in sortable table
- Show vulnerability details inline
- Support expanding/collapsing details
- Color-code by vulnerability severity
- Provide loading indicators
- Show summary statistics

## Best Practices

### Performance
- Use database indexes for common queries
- Batch API calls when possible
- Limit query results appropriately
- Cache when beneficial (database serves as cache)

### Maintainability
- Keep modules focused on single responsibility
- Use clear naming conventions
- Document complex logic
- Keep functions small and testable
- Avoid deep nesting

### User Experience
- Provide clear feedback (loading states, error messages)
- Make actions intuitive
- Support keyboard navigation
- Ensure responsive design
- Maintain visual consistency

### Testing Considerations
- Code should be testable (pure functions where possible)
- Handle edge cases gracefully
- Validate inputs thoroughly
- Provide clear error messages

## Project Structure

```
sbom-manager/
├── lib/
│   ├── db.js          # Database operations
│   ├── github.js      # GitHub API client
│   ├── osv.js         # OSV API client
│   ├── sbom.js        # SBOM parsing
│   ├── auth.js        # Authentication
│   └── utils.js       # Utilities
├── public/
│   ├── index.html     # HTML structure
│   ├── style.css      # Styles with CSS variables
│   ├── app.js         # Alpine.js app
│   └── favicon.svg    # Favicon
├── routes.js          # API route handlers
├── server.js          # Express server setup
├── package.json       # Dependencies
└── .cursorrules       # This file
```

## Common Patterns

### API Endpoints
- GET `/api/repos` - List user's repositories
- GET `/api/repo` - Fetch SBOM from GitHub
- GET `/api/load-scan` - Load scan from database
- POST `/api/vulns` - Check vulnerabilities
- GET `/api/search-deps` - Search dependencies
- GET `/api/me` - Get current user

### Database Tables
- `users` - GitHub user information
- `repos` - Repository metadata
- `scans` - Scan history
- `dependencies` - Dependency records
- `vulnerabilities` - Vulnerability records

### Frontend State
- Use Alpine.js `x-data` for component state
- Keep state minimal and focused
- Use reactive properties for UI updates
- Handle loading and error states

## Notes

- The app is called "ScanBase"
- Supports both authenticated and unauthenticated usage
- Database is SQLite (sbom.db)
- Environment variables: PORT, GITHUB_TOKEN, GITHUB_CLIENT_ID, GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET, DEBUG
- Brand colors: dark backgrounds (#222, #1a1a1a), blue links (#0a58ca, #60a5fa)
- Vulnerability colors: critical (red), high (orange), medium (yellow), low (green)

